%I A056990 #26 Oct 29 2026 00:04:11
%S A056990 1,2,8,192,22272,11550720,25629327360,236229525504000,
%T A056990 8858686914082897920,1331751782100764385607680
%N A056990 Number of nonsingular n X n {-1,1}-matrices.
%C A056990 Nonsingular matrices are exactly those with a nonzero determinant. They are invertible if the determinant is, hence not always unless the ring of coefficients is a field. For any such {-1,1}-matrix, multiplying any row or column by -1 gives another nonsingular matrix. We can therefore consider the subset S of these matrices which have only +1 in the first row and column, and then a(n) = 2^(n + n-1) * #S. For any matrix in S, we can subtract the first row from all others, so that all other elements will be either 0 or -2. If we factor out (-2)^(n-1), there remains a nonsingular {0,1}-matrix in rows and columns 2..n, therefore #S = A055165(n-1). - _M. F. Hasler_, Oct 26 2026
%H A056990 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/NonsingularMatrix.html">Nonsingular Matrix</a>.
%F A056990 a(n) = 2^(2*n-1) * A055165(n-1) for n>=1.
%e A056990 For n = 1, both matrices [1] and [-1] are nonsingular (and invertible).
%e A056990 For n = 2, one may choose any three matrix entries freely in +-1 (which gives a(2) = 2^3 = 8 possibilities); the 4th element must be the opposite of the product of the other three, to avoid collinear rows and zero determinant.
%e A056990 For n = 3, there are 2^(3+2) = 32 ways to choose the sign of the elements of the first row and column; factoring out the negative signs and subtracting the first row from the other rows leaves in rows & columns 2..3 one of the A055165(2) = 6 regular {0,1}-matrices of size 2 X 2 shown in A055165, multiplied by (-2). Going the reverse way gives 2^32 distinct {-1,1}-matrices for each of the 6 regular 2 X 2 {0,1}-matrices shown there. Hence a(3) = 6*32 = 192.
%o A056990 (PARI) apply( {A056990(n)=if(n, A088389(n--)*n!<<(2*n+1), 1)}, [0..5]) \\ _M. F. Hasler_, Oct 26 2026
%Y A056990 Cf. A055165, A056989.
